    container.querySelectorAll("input[type='radio']").forEach((input) =&gt; {
      input.checked = false;
    });
    feedbackNodes.forEach((feedback) =&gt; {
      feedback.textContent = "";
      feedback.className = "fcpython-quiz-feedback";
    });
    stepButtons.forEach((_, questionIndex) =&gt; {
      setStepStatus(questionIndex, "");
    });
    score.textContent = "";
    showQuestion(0, true);
  });

  actions.appendChild(previous);
  actions.appendChild(next);
  actions.appendChild(check);
  actions.appendChild(reset);
  container.appendChild(actions);
  container.appendChild(score);
  showQuestion(0);
  return container;
}

Teaching principles






TipKey idea



Teach by doing
Use this cycle repeatedly:

explain the purpose;
show a Example 1;
ask learners to predict the output;
run the code;
ask learners to explain it;
make one small change;
give a challenge with a hidden solution path.



This active-learning rhythm is inspired by The Carpentries lesson style: predictable episodes, setup checks, short challenges, and instructor-facing notes. The goal is not to copy their lessons, but to apply the same teaching wisdom across this Python course portfolio.

Change the teaching mode as learners progress

In Foundations, name and demonstrate the language or tool being learned, then ask learners to predict, modify, and debug it.
In the planned Intermediate course, present an unfamiliar problem without naming the feature to use. Ask learners to parse, model, solve, test, refactor, and explain.
In the planned Advanced course, add state, scale, performance, architecture, or concurrency constraints and require evidence for each trade-off.

Do not label a lesson advanced merely because it teaches decorators, generators, context managers, typing, or OOP. Those remain parts of a strong Python foundation; learner level is determined by problem complexity and independence.

