6. Recognize infinity and NaN at numeric boundaries
Some calculations or external numeric systems produce special floats:
positive_infinity = float("inf")
not_a_number = float("nan")

print(positive_infinity)
print(not_a_number)
Infinity is larger than ordinary finite floats:
print(float("inf") &gt; 1e308)
NaN means “not a number” and deliberately does not compare equal to itself:
not_a_number = float("nan")
print(not_a_number == not_a_number)
The result is False. Do not use equality to detect NaN. The math module has explicit checks:
import math

print(math.isfinite(12.5))
print(math.isfinite(float("inf")))
print(math.isnan(float("nan")))
Ordinary division by zero in Python still raises ZeroDivisionError; it does not automatically produce infinity:
result = 1.0 / 0.0
Special values often arrive from scientific libraries or external data. Recognize them now; later data and scientific courses will establish policies for them.

