4. A successful conversion can still lose information
Conversion does not promise that every detail survives:
measurement = 8.9
whole_part = int(measurement)

print(whole_part)
print(measurement)
int(8.9) produces 8. It truncates toward zero; it does not round to the nearest integer. The original measurement remains 8.9, but whole_part no longer records the fractional part.
Compare positive and negative values:
print(int(3.9))
print(int(-3.9))
The results are 3 and -3. Both move toward zero. If a ticket rule says “always use another seat for any partial group,” int() is the wrong operation. Lesson 2 will distinguish truncation, floor division, and rounding rules.

Numeric conversions can change the available precision
large_count = 10_000_000_000_000_001
approximate_count = float(large_count)
restored_count = int(approximate_count)

print(large_count)
print(approximate_count)
print(restored_count)
print(restored_count == large_count)
On standard Python, converting this large integer to a float cannot preserve every digit. Converting it back does not recover the lost information. A round trip is not evidence of safety unless the representation can express the original value.


bool() answers truthiness, not human yes/no text
print(bool("yes"))
print(bool("no"))
print(bool("False"))
print(bool(""))
The first three are True because every non-empty string is truthy. Only the empty string is false. Do not use bool(user_text) to parse a human answer. Lesson 7 builds an explicit yes/no rule.


