6. None records deliberate absence
Suppose a registration form allows a nickname but does not require one:
nickname = None
print(nickname is None)
None is a single special value used to represent “no value here” according to the program’s contract. It is not the text "None", not zero, and not an empty string.
Compare four possible states:
unknown_score = None
zero_score = 0
blank_note = ""
literal_word = "None"

print(unknown_score is None)
print(zero_score is None)
print(blank_note is None)
print(literal_word is None)
Only the first result is True. Zero may be a real score. An empty string may mean the person submitted a blank note. The word "None" is ordinary text.

Check None with identity
Use:
delivery_date = None
has_delivery_date = delivery_date is not None
print(has_delivery_date)
Python documentation and style guidance recommend is None and is not None. Lesson 6 of the course later examines equality and identity in depth. For now, treat this spelling as the standard None check.


Do not convert absence into misleading text
special_request = None
request_text = str(special_request)
print(request_text)
print(type(request_text).__name__)
The output looks like None, but request_text is now a four-character string. It no longer represents absence. Convert to display text only at the display boundary, and choose a human message deliberately:
special_request = None
display_request = "No special request"
print(display_request)
Later decision lessons will select that display based on the value. The key idea here is that representation carries meaning.


