4. / and // answer different questions
Even when both operands are integers, / produces a float:
print(12 / 3)
print(type(12 / 3).__name__)
print(12 // 3)
print(type(12 // 3).__name__)
The outputs are 4.0, float, 4, and int. Use / for a ratio or average and // for complete floor-division groups.

Average is not a complete-group count
total_points = 25
players = 4

average_points = total_points / players
complete_points_each = total_points // players

print(average_points)
print(complete_points_each)
The average is 6.25. If points must be indivisible objects distributed equally, each player can receive six complete points with one left over. The correct operator comes from the requirement.


Division by zero has no numeric result
Run this in its own cell:
groups = 0
items_per_group = 12 // groups
Python raises ZeroDivisionError. Changing groups to 1 removes the exception, but a real program must ask why zero reached a value used as a divisor. Unit 8 will teach controlled handling; for now, preserve the message and repair the source or stated rule.
The remainder operator also rejects a zero divisor:
remainder = 12 % 0
The failure belongs to the operation, not to the integer 12 by itself.


