2. Compare compatible meanings, not merely compatible syntax
Python can compare integers and floats numerically:
print(4 == 4.0)
print(3 &lt; 3.5)
Both are True. The types differ, but they participate in a common numeric comparison.
Boolean values have a historical numeric relationship:
print(True == 1)
print(False == 0)
print(True + True)
The results are True, True, and 2. Recognize this behavior, but do not use Boolean arithmetic when the program means a count. Give counts integer names and facts Boolean names.

String ordering is lexicographic by Unicode code points
print("apple" &lt; "banana")
print("Zoo" &lt; "apple")
print("10" &lt; "2")
Python compares the first differing character. Uppercase and lowercase code points have their own order, and digit strings are compared as text. "10" &lt; "2" is True because the first character "1" comes before "2".
Convert numeric text before a numeric comparison:
left_text = "10"
right_text = "2"

print(int(left_text) &lt; int(right_text))
The result is False, matching the numeric question.


Unrelated ordering raises an error
print("10" &lt; 2)
Python raises TypeError rather than guessing whether to compare text or numbers. Equality has a different contract and normally returns False for such unrelated values:
print("10" == 10)
That does not mean the values are “nearly equal.” It means they are not equal under Python’s value rules.


Float comparisons need the precision contract
import math

calculated = 0.1 + 0.2

print(calculated == 0.3)
print(math.isclose(calculated, 0.3))
Lesson 3 established the distinction: exact equality and closeness answer different questions.


