3. Truthiness asks whether a value counts as false in context
bool(value) reveals a value’s truthiness:
print(bool(0))
print(bool(7))
print(bool(-2))
print(bool(0.0))
Numeric zero is falsy. Other numbers are truthy, including negative numbers.
Strings follow emptiness:
print(bool(""))
print(bool("hello"))
print(bool(" "))
print(bool("False"))
Only the empty string is falsy. A space is a character, and the word "False" is non-empty text; both are truthy.
None is falsy:
print(bool(None))
Collections use emptiness too. These are previews of Unit 3:
print(bool([]))
print(bool([0]))
print(bool({}))
print(bool({"ready": False}))
An empty list or dictionary is falsy. A collection containing a falsy item is still non-empty and therefore truthy.

Truthiness can collapse different meanings
unknown_score = None
zero_score = 0
blank_score = ""

print(bool(unknown_score))
print(bool(zero_score))
print(bool(blank_score))
All three are falsy, but they can mean “not measured,” “measured as zero,” and “submitted as blank.” Use an explicit comparison when the distinction matters:
score = 0

has_score = score is not None
score_is_positive = score &gt; 0

print(has_score)
print(score_is_positive)
The program has a score, but it is not positive.


Parse human Boolean text with an explicit rule
This is wrong for yes/no input:
answer_text = "no"
answer = bool(answer_text)
print(answer)
It prints True. Instead, clean and compare the accepted spelling:
answer_text = "  YES "
answer = answer_text.strip().casefold() == "yes"
print(answer)
This simple rule recognizes only yes as true; every other spelling produces False. A production form may need to reject unknown answers rather than treating them as no. Exception and validation units will design that boundary explicitly.


